      "admissionsTests": {"status":"verified","none":true,"sources":[{"url":"https://undergrad.admissions.columbia.edu/apply/firstyear","label":"Columbia: First-Year Applicants"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":"In addition to the Coalition Application and the Common Application, you must also respond to Columbia-specific questions to provide the Committee on Admissions with a fuller sense of you as a person—including your academic, extracurricular and intellectual interests.","note":"No course-specific admissions test for Columbia College or Columbia Engineering; the first-year application (Common/Coalition Application plus Columbia-specific questions) is the same for both."},
      "subjectPrerequisites": {"status":"partial","items":["Not used in Altiora’s matching yet — see the note for the official guidance"],"codes":[],"sources":[{"url":"https://undergrad.admissions.columbia.edu/apply/firstyear","label":"Columbia: First-Year Applicants"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":"Columbia Engineering: The School of Engineering and Applied Science prescribes no standardized course of study for secondary school students applying for first-year admission. However, the Engineering curriculum is heavily driven by physics and calculus, so Columbia Engineering strongly advises these courses within the following recommended academic preparation: Four years of mathematics through calculus—unless you have already completed what is available at your school Four years of laboratory science, including one year of chemistry and one year of physics—preferably in the last two years of secondary school","note":"Official (Columbia Engineering): no standardized course of study is prescribed, but Columbia Engineering strongly advises physics and calculus within its recommended preparation — four years of mathematics through calculus (unless already completed what is available), four years of laboratory science including one year of chemistry and one year of physics (preferably in the last two years), four years of English literature and composition, three to four years of history/social studies, and two to three years of a world language.","reviewFlag":"Official source lists strongly advised high-school preparation for Columbia Engineering (mathematics through calculus; laboratory science incl. one year each of chemistry and physics); data/courseRequirements.js holds none. Owner decision."},
